{"name":"Bright Manatee","hex":"#979aaa","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bright","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Emitting much light; visually dazzling; luminous, lucent, radiant.","Of light: brilliant, intense."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Brightness, glow.\n\n Glory, splendour.","Brightness, glow."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bright"},{"term":"manatee","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mostly herbivorous sirenian of the genus Trichechus, having a paddle-like tail and nails on their flippers, found in tropical regions."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/manatee"}],"entries":[{"title":"Manatee","blocks":[{"text":"Manatees are large, fully aquatic, mostly herbivorous marine mammals sometimes known as sea cows. There are three accepted living species of Trichechidae, representing three of the four living species in the order Sirenia: the Amazonian manatee, the West Indian manatee, and the West African manatee. They measure up to 4.0 metres long, weigh as much as 590 kilograms (1,300 lb), and have paddle-like tails."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Underwater_photography_on_endangered_mammal_manatee.jpg/330px-Underwater_photography_on_endangered_mammal_manatee.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manatee","wikidata":"Q42797"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Manatee_skeleton_with_calf.jpg/500px-Manatee_skeleton_with_calf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Manatee_skeleton_with_calf.jpg/500px-Manatee_skeleton_with_calf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A skeleton of a manatee and calf, the Museum of Osteology, Oklahoma City","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File